Today we're focusing on something every office worker needs to know.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,180
Professional email phrases for business conversations.

12
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:38,640
Emails are a huge part of business communication.

13
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:47,170
Whether you work in an office, work remotely, run a business, or communicate with clients, you probably send emails every single day.

14
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:51,200
And sometimes writing emails in English can feel stressful.

15
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:52,010
Exactly.

16
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:57,340
Many English learners ask themselves questions like, how do I sound professional?

17
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:59,440
How do I write polite emails?

18
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:02,740
What phrases do native speakers actually use?

19
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:04,240
Well, don't worry.

20
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:12,730
In today's episode, we'll teach you useful professional email phrases, explain their meanings, and show you practical examples through natural conversations.

21
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:18,220
So grab your notebook, relax, and let's improve your business English together.

22
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:21,310
All right, Sophie, let's start with the beginning of an email.

23
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:22,250
Good idea.

24
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:27,620
One of the most common professional opening phrases is, I hope you're doing well.

25
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:28,940
Very common.

26
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:29,920
Very polite.

27
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:31,460
The meaning is simple.

28
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:35,610
It's a friendly way to begin an email before discussing business matters.

29
00:01:36,230 --> 00:01:37,610
Example conversation time.

30
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:41,550
Daniel, I need to email a client I haven't spoken to in a while.

31
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:42,710
How should I start?

32
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:45,240
You can write, I hope you're doing well.

33
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,260
It sounds warm and professional.

34
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:47,820
Nice.

35
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:53,790
Another useful opening phrase is, I'm writing regarding… Excellent business phrase.

36
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:58,030
The meaning is, I'm contacting you about a specific topic.

37
00:01:58,620 --> 00:01:59,700
Example conversation.

38
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,540
Sophie, how do I begin an email about tomorrow's meeting?

39
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:07,670
You can say, I'm writing regarding tomorrow's project meeting.

40
00:02:08,230 --> 00:02:09,790
Very professional and direct.

41
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:14,040
Another useful phrase is, I wanted to follow up on…

42
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:14,700
Oh, yes.

43
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,800
Native speakers use this constantly.

44
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:21,730
The meaning is, I want to check on the progress or continue a previous discussion.

45
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:23,070
Practical example.

46
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:24,110
Hi, Sophie.

47
00:02:24,390 --> 00:02:27,640
I sent the proposal last week, but the client hasn't replied yet.

48
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:33,730
Then you can send another email saying, I wanted to follow up on the proposal I shared last Tuesday.

49
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:35,750
Great professional communication.

50
00:02:36,310 --> 00:02:39,580
Another important phrase is, please find attached.

51
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:41,900
Essential email English.

52
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:45,070
The meaning is, the file is attached to the email.

53
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:46,590
Example conversation.

54
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:49,980
Daniel, how do I tell someone I attached the report?

55
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:54,090
You can write, please find attached the updated sales report.

56
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:56,170
Very formal and professional.

57
00:02:56,690 --> 00:02:59,680
Another phrase I use often is, could you please?

58
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,620
Super important for polite requests.

59
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:06,130
The meaning is simply asking someone to do something politely.

60
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:07,720
Example conversation.

61
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:11,960
Sophie, I need the finance department to send me the latest numbers.

62
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:17,530
Then you could write, could you please send me the updated financial figures by this afternoon?

63
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:21,040
Much more professional than just saying, send me the numbers.

64
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:22,100
Exactly.

65
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,720
Politeness matters in business emails.

66
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:28,390
Another useful phrase is, I'd appreciate it if you...

67
00:03:28,990 --> 00:03:31,190
Excellent phrase for formal requests.

68
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:34,360
The meaning is, I would be thankful if you could do this.

69
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:36,000
Example conversation.

70
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:40,170
I need my manager to review my presentation before the meeting.

71
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:45,670
You could write, I'd appreciate it if you could review the presentation before tomorrow morning.

72
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:47,600
That sounds very respectful.

73
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:51,560
Another common business phrase is just to clarify.

74
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:54,210
Very useful in professional communication.

75
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:59,470
The meaning is, I want to make something clearer to avoid misunderstanding.

76
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:00,720
Example conversation.

77
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:04,250
Daniel, I'm confused about the deadline in this email.

78
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:09,580
Then you can reply, just to clarify, is the final deadline Friday or Monday?

79
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,140
Nice and professional.

80
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:15,490
Another phrase native speakers use constantly is please let me know.

81
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:16,470
Oh, yes.

82
00:04:16,970 --> 00:04:20,110
That phrase appears in almost every office email.

83
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:22,590
The meaning is, please inform me.

84
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:24,190
Example conversation.

85
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:27,580
Sophie, how can I politely ask for confirmation?

86
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:31,360
You can write, please let me know if the meeting time works for you.

87
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,490
Very natural business English.

88
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:36,970
Another important phrase is, as discussed.

89
00:04:37,610 --> 00:04:39,930
Excellent for summarizing previous conversations.

90
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:43,520
The meaning is, as we talked about earlier.

91
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:44,870
Example conversation.

92
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:47,520
I spoke with a client on the phone this morning.

93
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,740
Now I need to send a summary email.

94
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:53,670
Then you can start with, as discussed during our call this morning.

95
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:55,350
Great professional style.

96
00:04:55,950 --> 00:04:59,170
Another useful email phrase is, I apologize for.

97
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:02,340
Very important for professional apologies.

98
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,200
The meaning is, I'm sorry for something.

99
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:06,840
Example conversation.

100
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:10,670
I forgot to reply to an important client email.

101
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:14,210
Then you can write, I apologize for the delayed response.

102
00:05:14,770 --> 00:05:17,090
Much more professional than just saying, sorry.

103
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:18,150
Exactly.

104
00:05:18,690 --> 00:05:22,440
Another professional phrase used often is, thank you for your patience.

105
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,460
Great customer service phrase.

106
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,760
The meaning is, thanking someone for waiting calmly.

107
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:29,320
Example conversation.

108
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:32,050
The project update took longer than expected.

109
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:36,650
Then you can write, thank you for your patience while we finalized the report.

110
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:38,940
Very polite and professional.

111
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:42,590
Another important phrase is, I'm happy to inform you.

112
00:05:43,190 --> 00:05:44,600
Excellent phrase for good news.

113
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:47,800
The meaning is, I'm pleased to tell you.

114
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:49,400
Example conversation.

115
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:52,630
Daniel, how can I announce a promotion by email?

116
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:57,960
You can write, I'm happy to inform you that you've been promoted to senior project manager.

117
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:00,200
That sounds professional and positive.

118
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:03,490
Another common phrase is, unfortunately.

119
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:04,320
Ah, yes.

120
00:06:04,990 --> 00:06:07,490
Useful for delivering bad news politely.

121
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:09,070
Example conversation.

122
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:11,140
The shipment won't arrive today.

123
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:16,470
Then you can write, unfortunately, the shipment has been delayed due to weather conditions.

124
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:18,910
Much softer and more professional.

125
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:23,280
Another phrase people use frequently is at your earliest convenience.

126
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:25,850
Very formal business English.

127
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:29,290
The meaning is when you have time as soon as possible.

128
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:30,850
Example conversation.

129
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:34,620
Sophie, how can I politely ask for a document quickly?

130
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:39,180
You can say, please send the signed contract at your earliest convenience.

131
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:41,130
Excellent professional tone.

132
00:06:41,710 --> 00:06:44,470
Another useful phrase is I'm reaching out to.

133
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:46,550
Very modern business English.

134
00:06:47,110 --> 00:06:50,060
The meaning is I'm contacting you for a reason.

135
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:51,740
Example conversation.

136
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:55,240
Daniel, I want to ask a supplier about pricing.

137
00:06:55,490 --> 00:07:00,020
Then you can write, I'm reaching out to inquire about your latest pricing options.

138
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:01,680
Great business phrase.

139
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:06,170
Another professional phrase is, please don't hesitate to contact me.

140
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:08,010
Excellent closing phrase.

141
00:07:08,570 --> 00:07:11,500
The meaning is, feel free to contact me anytime.

142
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:13,060
Example conversation.

143
00:07:13,620 --> 00:07:16,550
Sophie, how do I sound friendly at the end of an email?

144
00:07:17,070 --> 00:07:21,330
You can write, please don't hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

145
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:23,300
Very professional and welcoming.

146
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:26,880
Another useful phrase is looking forward to hearing from you.

147
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:29,550
One of the most common email closings.

148
00:07:30,110 --> 00:07:32,410
The meaning is I hope you reply soon.

149
00:07:32,970 --> 00:07:34,050
Example conversation.

150
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:37,740
Daniel, I just sent a partnership proposal to a company.

151
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:38,500
Great.

152
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:42,190
You can end the email with looking forward to hearing from you soon.

153
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:44,070
Very smooth and natural.

154
00:07:44,610 --> 00:07:48,860
Another phrase professionals use all the time is thank you for your understanding.

155
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:51,710
Very useful during difficult situations.

156
00:07:52,310 --> 00:07:56,090
The meaning is thanking someone for being reasonable and cooperative.

157
00:07:56,630 --> 00:07:57,670
Example conversation.

158
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:01,140
Our office will close early tomorrow because of maintenance work.

159
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:05,280
Then you can write, thank you for your understanding and cooperation.

160
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:07,190
Excellent professional English.

161
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:10,750
Another phrase I love is, please keep me updated.

162
00:08:11,330 --> 00:08:13,590
Very common in team communication.

163
00:08:13,610 --> 00:08:16,920
The meaning is, continue informing me about progress.

164
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:18,640
Example conversation.

165
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,640
The software team is fixing a technical issue today.

166
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:26,010
Then a manager might write, please keep me updated on the progress.

167
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:27,570
Great workplace phrase.

168
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:31,570
Another professional expression is, I'd like to schedule a meeting.

169
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:33,460
Essential business English.

170
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:35,040
Example conversation.

171
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:38,580
Daniel, I need to discuss a new project with a client.

172
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:43,410
Then you write, I'd like to schedule a meeting to discuss the upcoming project.

173
00:08:43,910 --> 00:08:45,130
Nice and professional.

174
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:48,920
Another useful phrase is, please feel free to share your thoughts.

175
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:50,940
Excellent collaborative phrase.

176
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:54,370
The meaning is, you are welcome to give your opinions.

177
00:08:54,930 --> 00:08:55,950
Example conversation.

178
00:08:56,490 --> 00:08:59,750
Sophie, I'm sending the first draft of a proposal to my team.

179
00:09:00,330 --> 00:09:04,000
Then you can write, please feel free to share your thoughts and suggestions.

180
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:06,000
Great teamwork communication.

181
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:09,190
Another common phrase is, I'll keep you posted.

182
00:09:09,850 --> 00:09:11,350
Very natural business English.

183
00:09:11,910 --> 00:09:14,720
The meaning is, I'll continue updating you.

184
00:09:14,810 --> 00:09:15,930
Example conversation.

185
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:18,360
Has the client made a final decision yet?

186
00:09:18,920 --> 00:09:22,060
Not yet, but I'll keep you posted as soon as I hear back from them.

187
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:23,860
Excellent office phrase.

188
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:28,790
Wow, Sophie, we covered so many useful professional email phrases today.

189
00:09:29,370 --> 00:09:30,090
We really did.

190
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:34,690
And these are phrases you can start using immediately in your daily office emails.

191
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:40,620
Remember, professional emails should sound polite, clear, respectful, and confident.

192
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:45,210
The more you practice these phrases, the more natural your business English will become.

193
00:09:45,770 --> 00:09:48,770
Try writing your own example emails using these expressions.

194
00:09:49,130 --> 00:09:51,270
That's one of the best ways to improve quickly.

195
00:09:51,770 --> 00:09:53,480
Thanks so much for learning with us today.

196
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:58,260
We hope this episode helps you feel more confident when writing business emails in English.

197
00:09:58,830 --> 00:10:02,690
Keep practicing, stay motivated, and enjoy your English learning journey.

