And I'm Sophia.

203
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:28,670
Today's topic is professional email phrases for business conversations.

204
00:10:29,350 --> 00:10:33,880
In modern workplaces, emails are one of the most important communication tools.

205
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:39,430
A well-written email can help you sound professional, organized, polite, and confident.

206
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:44,000
But many English learners struggle with choosing the right words and expressions.

207
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:47,180
That's exactly why today's episode is practical.

208
00:10:47,830 --> 00:10:56,760
We'll introduce useful professional email phrases, explain their meanings, and show you real workplace conversations in situations where people actually use them.

209
00:10:57,380 --> 00:11:01,250
So let's begin with one of the most common professional email phrases.

210
00:11:01,550 --> 00:11:04,450
The phrase is, I hope this email finds you well.

211
00:11:05,130 --> 00:11:08,420
Meaning, this is a polite way to begin a professional email.

212
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:12,680
It's friendly, respectful, and commonly used in business communication.

213
00:11:13,350 --> 00:11:14,870
Practical usage conversation.

214
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:18,610
Sophia, I noticed you always start your emails professionally.

215
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:19,520
Yes.

216
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:23,500
One phrase I often use is, I hope this email finds you well.

217
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:26,350
It sounds warm and professional at the same time.

218
00:11:27,050 --> 00:11:27,490
Exactly.

219
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:30,270
Especially when emailing clients or managers.

220
00:11:30,690 --> 00:11:32,330
Here's an example email opening.

221
00:11:33,050 --> 00:11:35,980
Dear Mr. Johnson, I hope this email finds you well.

222
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:38,560
I'm writing regarding next week's project meeting.

223
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:40,680
Very professional and natural.

224
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,430
Let's continue with another useful phrase.

225
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:45,510
I'm writing to inform you that.

226
00:11:46,170 --> 00:11:49,370
Meaning, you are officially giving information or updates.

227
00:11:50,030 --> 00:11:51,620
This phrase is clear and direct.

228
00:11:52,340 --> 00:11:53,380
Example conversation.

229
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:57,220
Daniel, how would you announce a schedule change professionally?

230
00:11:57,610 --> 00:12:05,520
I'd probably write, I'm writing to inform you that tomorrow's meeting has been rescheduled to 3 p.m. That sounds very professional.

231
00:12:06,220 --> 00:12:07,400
It's simple but effective.

232
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:11,130
Another important phrase is, please find attached.

233
00:12:11,830 --> 00:12:15,230
Meaning, the email includes a file or document attachment.

234
00:12:15,830 --> 00:12:18,280
This phrase is extremely common in offices.

235
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:20,060
Example conversation.

236
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:22,600
Did you send the quarterly report yet?

237
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:24,520
Yes, I emailed it this morning.

238
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:25,740
What did you write?

239
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:29,960
Please find attached the updated quarterly sales report for your review.

240
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:31,950
Perfect professional wording.

241
00:12:32,590 --> 00:12:34,450
Here's another practical email phrase.

242
00:12:34,910 --> 00:12:36,750
Please let me know if you have any questions.

243
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:40,060
Meaning, you are offering help or clarification.

244
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:42,680
This phrase sounds polite and supportive.

245
00:12:43,350 --> 00:12:44,390
Example conversation.

246
00:12:45,010 --> 00:12:49,080
Whenever I send instructions to new employees, I always end with that phrase.

247
00:12:49,700 --> 00:12:50,280
Why?

248
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:53,350
because it encourages communication and sounds approachable.

249
00:12:53,950 --> 00:12:54,430
That's true.

250
00:12:55,110 --> 00:12:56,930
Example email sentence.

251
00:12:56,950 --> 00:13:00,410
Please let me know if you have any questions regarding the onboarding process.

252
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:02,000
Very professional.

253
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:04,960
Next phrase, I'd like to follow up on.

254
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:09,550
Meaning, you are checking for updates about a previous discussion or email.

255
00:13:10,190 --> 00:13:11,270
Example conversation.

256
00:13:11,850 --> 00:13:14,290
Clients sometimes forget to reply to emails.

257
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:15,520
Exactly.

258
00:13:15,860 --> 00:13:17,900
That's when follow-up emails become important.

259
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:19,520
What would you write?

260
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:23,710
I'd like to follow up on my previous email regarding the marketing proposal.

261
00:13:24,330 --> 00:13:25,510
Polite and professional.

262
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:29,740
Another excellent phrase is thank you for your prompt response.

263
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,080
Meaning you appreciate someone for replying quickly.

264
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:34,860
Example conversation.

265
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:37,830
Fast communication is important in business.

266
00:13:38,570 --> 00:13:39,210
Absolutely.

267
00:13:39,890 --> 00:13:40,730
Example sentence.

268
00:13:41,430 --> 00:13:42,810
Thank you for your prompt response.

269
00:13:43,150 --> 00:13:44,500
I appreciate your assistance.

270
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:46,440
That sounds excellent.

271
00:13:46,650 --> 00:13:48,680
Here's another practical business phrase.

272
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:50,260
Please could you confirm?

273
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:54,020
Meaning you are asking someone to verify information.

274
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:55,780
Example conversation.

275
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:59,430
We often need confirmation before scheduling meetings.

276
00:14:00,150 --> 00:14:00,410
Right.

277
00:14:00,890 --> 00:14:04,720
For example, could you please confirm your availability for Friday's meeting?

278
00:14:05,340 --> 00:14:06,440
Clear and professional.

279
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:11,540
Another very important email phrase is I apologize for the inconvenience.

280
00:14:12,250 --> 00:14:15,530
Meaning you are apologizing for a problem or delay.

281
00:14:15,930 --> 00:14:17,070
Example conversation.

282
00:14:17,730 --> 00:14:20,260
Sometimes mistakes happen at work.

283
00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:21,380
They definitely do.

284
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:23,940
The key is handling them professionally.

285
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:26,790
What would you write if there was a delivery delay?

286
00:14:27,550 --> 00:14:30,720
I apologize for the inconvenience caused by the shipping delay.

287
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:32,620
Excellent business English.

288
00:14:33,320 --> 00:14:36,900
Let's continue with another practical phrase professionals use daily.

289
00:14:37,550 --> 00:14:38,950
At your earliest convenience.

290
00:14:39,650 --> 00:14:43,250
Meaning whenever possible or as soon as you reasonably can.

291
00:14:43,640 --> 00:14:46,080
It sounds more polite than saying immediately.

292
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:47,830
Example conversation.

293
00:14:48,490 --> 00:14:51,350
How can we politely ask someone to review a document?

294
00:14:51,930 --> 00:14:55,740
You can say, please review the attached document at your earliest convenience.

295
00:14:56,420 --> 00:14:58,160
That sounds respectful and professional.

296
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:01,930
Here's another practical phrase I appreciate your understanding.

297
00:15:02,570 --> 00:15:05,970
Meaning, you thank someone for being patient or cooperative.

298
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:07,760
Example conversation.

299
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:11,720
Let's say a project deadline changes unexpectedly.

300
00:15:12,090 --> 00:15:16,900
Then I might write, we appreciate your understanding regarding the updated timeline.

301
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:19,220
Very natural workplace English.

302
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:23,290
Another extremely common phrase is, I'm happy to assist.

303
00:15:23,930 --> 00:15:25,510
Meaning, you are willing to help.

304
00:15:26,210 --> 00:15:28,070
Example email sentence.

305
00:15:28,090 --> 00:15:30,840
If you need additional information, I'm happy to assist.

306
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:32,700
Professional and friendly.

307
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:35,920
Next phrase, please don't hesitate to contact me.

308
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:39,750
Meaning, feel free to communicate anytime if help is needed.

309
00:15:40,470 --> 00:15:41,690
Example conversation.

310
00:15:41,990 --> 00:15:44,780
This phrase is common at the end of professional emails.

311
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:48,820
Yes, especially in customer service and management communication.

312
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:50,290
Example sentence.

313
00:15:50,930 --> 00:15:54,270
Please don't hesitate to contact me if you require further assistance.

314
00:15:54,930 --> 00:15:55,350
Excellent.

315
00:15:56,070 --> 00:15:59,200
Another useful phrase is I'd appreciate your feedback.

316
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:02,480
Meaning you want someone's opinion or suggestions.

317
00:16:03,180 --> 00:16:04,210
Example conversation.

318
00:16:04,830 --> 00:16:06,950
Feedback is important in team projects.

319
00:16:07,770 --> 00:16:08,330
Definitely.

320
00:16:08,990 --> 00:16:10,940
How would you ask for it professionally?

321
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:14,510
I'd appreciate your feedback on the revised presentation slides.

322
00:16:15,230 --> 00:16:16,590
Very clear and professional.

323
00:16:17,230 --> 00:16:19,390
Here's another phrase managers use often.

324
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:21,960
Let's schedule a meeting to discuss this further.

325
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:24,480
Meaning, more discussion is needed.

326
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:26,200
Example conversation.

327
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:30,090
Sometimes email conversations become too complicated.

328
00:16:30,810 --> 00:16:31,330
Exactly.

329
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:33,310
That's when meetings help.

330
00:16:33,330 --> 00:16:34,130
Example sentence.

331
00:16:34,820 --> 00:16:37,540
Let's schedule a meeting next week to discuss this further.

332
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:39,860
Perfect business communication.

333
00:16:40,180 --> 00:16:43,680
Another practical phrase is, thank you for bringing this to my attention.

334
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:47,790
Meaning, thank you for informing me about the issue or situation.

335
00:16:48,470 --> 00:16:49,510
Example conversation.

336
00:16:50,150 --> 00:16:52,720
Employees often report problems to managers.

337
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:53,640
Right.

338
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:58,000
A professional manager might respond with, thank you for bringing this to my attention.

339
00:16:58,260 --> 00:16:59,880
I'll look into the matter immediately.

340
00:17:00,530 --> 00:17:02,450
That sounds responsible and professional.

341
00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:04,870
Next phrase, as discussed.

342
00:17:05,510 --> 00:17:08,940
Meaning, referring to a previous conversation or meeting.

343
00:17:09,250 --> 00:17:10,410
Example conversation.

344
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,200
This phrase helps remind people about agreements.

345
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:14,660
Exactly.

346
00:17:15,140 --> 00:17:15,940
Example sentence.

347
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:20,350
As discussed during today's meeting, the project deadline has been extended.

348
00:17:21,070 --> 00:17:22,770
Very useful for business emails.

349
00:17:23,510 --> 00:17:26,750
Another important phrase is I'm looking forward to your response.

350
00:17:27,370 --> 00:17:29,750
Meaning you politely expect a reply.

351
00:17:30,430 --> 00:17:31,440
Example conversation.

352
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:34,400
That phrase sounds both polite and professional.

353
00:17:35,020 --> 00:17:37,520
Yes, especially when communicating with clients.

354
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,140
Example sentence.

355
00:17:39,550 --> 00:17:42,190
I'm looking forward to your response regarding the proposal.

356
00:17:42,830 --> 00:17:43,170
Excellent.

357
00:17:43,810 --> 00:17:45,390
Here's another advanced phrase.

358
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:46,890
Please keep me updated.

359
00:17:47,510 --> 00:17:50,680
Meaning, continue sharing progress or new information.

360
00:17:51,380 --> 00:17:52,440
Example conversation.

361
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:55,760
Managers often say this during ongoing projects.

362
00:17:56,550 --> 00:17:57,180
Absolutely.

363
00:17:57,630 --> 00:17:58,450
Example sentence.

364
00:17:58,990 --> 00:18:01,230
Please keep me updated on the project's progress.

365
00:18:01,950 --> 00:18:03,570
Very common workplace English.

366
00:18:04,290 --> 00:18:07,260
Another professional phrase is I'd like to clarify.

367
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:11,590
Meaning you want to explain something clearly to avoid misunderstanding.

368
00:18:12,310 --> 00:18:13,390
Example conversation.

369
00:18:14,030 --> 00:18:16,950
Miscommunication can create big problems at work.

370
00:18:17,650 --> 00:18:19,580
That's why clarification is important.

371
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:21,060
Example sentence.

372
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:24,640
I'd like to clarify the payment terms mentioned in the agreement.

373
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:26,380
Excellent business English.

374
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:29,240
Let's continue with another practical phrase.

375
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:31,030
Thank you for your patience.

376
00:18:31,670 --> 00:18:34,230
Meaning you appreciate someone waiting calmly.

377
00:18:34,910 --> 00:18:36,130
Example conversation.

378
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:39,020
Customer service teams use this phrase frequently.

379
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,830
Yes, example sentence, thank you for your patience while we resolve the issue.

380
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:45,790
Professional and respectful.

381
00:18:46,450 --> 00:18:49,200
Another important phrase is we are currently reviewing.

382
00:18:49,820 --> 00:18:53,180
Meaning the company is examining or evaluating something.

383
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:54,920
Example conversation.

384
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:58,330
What if a client asks for an update you don't have yet?

385
00:18:59,070 --> 00:19:03,430
I'd write, we are currently reviewing your request and will provide an update shortly.

386
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:05,260
Very professional.

387
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:07,060
Here's another excellent phrase.

388
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:08,700
Please be advised that.

389
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:12,070
Meaning formal way to give important information.

390
00:19:12,790 --> 00:19:13,790
Example conversation.

391
00:19:14,430 --> 00:19:17,110
This phrase sounds more formal than everyday English.

392
00:19:17,830 --> 00:19:18,270
Exactly.

393
00:19:18,670 --> 00:19:19,450
Example sentence.

394
00:19:20,010 --> 00:19:22,820
Please be advised that our office will be closed on Monday.

395
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:24,560
Great professional tone.

396
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:28,040
Another useful phrase is thank you for your cooperation.

397
00:19:28,680 --> 00:19:31,060
Meaning you appreciate teamwork or support.

398
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:32,960
Example conversation.

399
00:19:33,330 --> 00:19:37,000
This phrase is common after completing projects or solving problems.

400
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:37,900
Right.

401
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:39,640
Example sentence.

402
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:42,070
Thank you for your cooperation throughout this project.

403
00:19:42,770 --> 00:19:44,350
Very natural business English.

404
00:19:45,110 --> 00:19:49,460
Finally, let's end with one of the most important professional email closings.

405
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:50,920
Best regards.

406
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:54,530
Meaning, a polite and professional way to end an email.

407
00:19:55,170 --> 00:19:56,210
Example conversation.

408
00:19:56,950 --> 00:19:59,260
What email closing do you use most often?

409
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:03,950
Usually best regards because it works in almost every professional situation.

410
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:05,090
I agree.

411
00:20:05,530 --> 00:20:07,980
It sounds polite, respectful, and professional.

412
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:09,360
Example ending.

413
00:20:09,900 --> 00:20:12,790
Best regards, Sophia Miller, Marketing Manager.

414
00:20:13,370 --> 00:20:13,790
Perfect.

415
00:20:14,390 --> 00:20:19,680
Today we covered many practical professional email phrases used in real business communication.

416
00:20:20,380 --> 00:20:24,910
These expressions can help you sound more confident, polished, and professional in English.

417
00:20:25,290 --> 00:20:31,440
The key is not only memorizing the phrases, but practicing them regularly in real workplace situations.

418
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:37,090
Try using these expressions in your next email to a colleague, manager or client.

419
00:20:37,110 --> 00:20:40,410
Over time, professional English writing will become natural for you.

420
00:20:41,090 --> 00:20:43,120
Thank you for spending time learning with us today.

421
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:47,120
We hope this episode helps improve your business communication skills.

422
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:51,990
Keep practicing, stay confident and continue improving your English every day.

423
00:20:52,380 --> 00:20:55,960
We'll see you again in the next Professional English Podcast.

424
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:57,700
Have a productive and successful day.

425
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:04,770
