427
00:21:14,420 --> 00:21:15,880
That sounds amazing, doesn't it?

428
00:21:16,360 --> 00:21:19,040
Today we're going to help you do exactly that.

429
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:23,740
Our topic today is professional email phrases for business conversations.

430
00:21:24,120 --> 00:21:31,750
In this episode, you'll learn useful email phrases, what they mean, and how professionals use them in real workplace situations.

431
00:21:32,260 --> 00:21:40,720
We'll practice polite requests, follow-up emails, scheduling meetings, giving updates, apologizing professionally, and much more.

432
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:47,270
These are the kinds of phrases native speakers use every day in international companies and business environments.

433
00:21:47,390 --> 00:21:53,800
So if you want your emails to sound more fluent, clear, and professional, this lesson is perfect for you.

434
00:21:54,190 --> 00:21:54,940
All right, everyone.

435
00:21:55,180 --> 00:21:58,060
Let's begin with our first professional email phrase.

436
00:21:58,620 --> 00:22:02,080
Our first phrase is, I hope this email finds you well.

437
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:06,410
This is one of the most common ways to begin a professional email politely.

438
00:22:06,450 --> 00:22:10,050
It's a friendly way to say you hope the other person is doing well.

439
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:11,550
Example conversation.

440
00:22:12,010 --> 00:22:13,620
I received your email this morning.

441
00:22:13,730 --> 00:22:14,050
Great.

442
00:22:14,390 --> 00:22:17,280
I started it with, I hope this email finds you well.

443
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:19,220
It sounds polite and professional.

444
00:22:19,680 --> 00:22:20,240
Absolutely.

445
00:22:20,500 --> 00:22:22,960
That's a very standard business email opening.

446
00:22:23,330 --> 00:22:24,690
Here's another useful phrase.

447
00:22:25,170 --> 00:22:26,690
I'm writing to inform you that.

448
00:22:27,230 --> 00:22:30,720
This phrase is used when giving official information or updates.

449
00:22:31,220 --> 00:22:32,280
Example conversation.

450
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:34,340
Why did you email the client today?

451
00:22:34,740 --> 00:22:39,450
I'm writing to inform you that the project deadline has been extended until next Friday.

452
00:22:39,490 --> 00:22:41,350
That sounds very professional.

453
00:22:41,790 --> 00:22:42,450
Definitely.

454
00:22:42,590 --> 00:22:44,970
Next phrase, please find attached.

455
00:22:45,390 --> 00:22:48,610
This means you attached a document or file to the email.

456
00:22:49,030 --> 00:22:50,110
Example conversation.

457
00:22:50,550 --> 00:22:52,340
Did you send the monthly report already?

458
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:56,680
Yes, I wrote, please find attached the final sales report for March.

459
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:58,240
Perfect business English.

460
00:22:58,820 --> 00:23:00,620
Very common in office communication.

461
00:23:01,060 --> 00:23:03,490
Next phrase, could you please confirm receipt?

462
00:23:03,930 --> 00:23:07,970
This means asking someone to confirm they received your email or document.

463
00:23:08,410 --> 00:23:09,670
Example conversation.

464
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:13,080
I sent the contract this morning, but I'm not sure they received it.

465
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:17,270
Then send a follow-up email and ask, could you please confirm receipt?

466
00:23:17,690 --> 00:23:18,150
Good idea.

467
00:23:18,590 --> 00:23:20,130
Very useful professional phrase.

468
00:23:20,590 --> 00:23:22,520
Here's another excellent email phrase.

469
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:24,620
I'd appreciate your feedback.

470
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,940
This means you want someone's opinion or suggestions.

471
00:23:28,510 --> 00:23:29,550
Example conversation.

472
00:23:29,930 --> 00:23:32,090
I finished the presentation draft yesterday.

473
00:23:32,490 --> 00:23:32,970
Great.

474
00:23:32,990 --> 00:23:36,980
Send it to the manager and say, I'd appreciate your feedback before the meeting.

475
00:23:37,130 --> 00:23:38,690
That sounds polite and professional.

476
00:23:39,130 --> 00:23:39,570
Exactly.

477
00:23:39,930 --> 00:23:43,170
Next phrase, just following up on my previous email.

478
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:47,800
Following up means checking again about something after waiting for a response.

479
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:49,360
Example conversation.

480
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:52,350
The client still hasn't replied to my proposal.

481
00:23:52,770 --> 00:23:57,980
Then send another email saying, just following up on my previous email regarding the proposal.

482
00:23:58,560 --> 00:23:59,420
Very professional.

483
00:23:59,800 --> 00:24:02,240
And extremely common in business communication.

484
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:06,090
Next phrase, please let me know if you have any questions.

485
00:24:06,270 --> 00:24:09,370
This means politely offering help or clarification.

486
00:24:09,870 --> 00:24:10,940
Example conversation.

487
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:13,760
I just sent detailed instructions to the team.

488
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:14,420
Good.

489
00:24:14,740 --> 00:24:16,420
Did you end the email professionally?

490
00:24:16,770 --> 00:24:17,070
Yes.

491
00:24:17,370 --> 00:24:20,170
I wrote, please let me know if you have any questions.

492
00:24:20,630 --> 00:24:21,010
Perfect.

493
00:24:21,370 --> 00:24:22,890
Great customer service phrase, too.

494
00:24:23,430 --> 00:24:23,940
Absolutely.

495
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:25,780
Here's another useful expression.

496
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:27,880
Thank you for your prompt response.

497
00:24:28,340 --> 00:24:30,610
Prompt means quick or fast.

498
00:24:30,630 --> 00:24:31,710
Example conversation.

499
00:24:31,750 --> 00:24:34,890
The supplier replied much faster than expected.

500
00:24:35,060 --> 00:24:38,940
Then thank them by writing, thank you for your prompt response.

501
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:40,430
That sounds very professional.

502
00:24:40,870 --> 00:24:41,350
Definitely.

503
00:24:41,810 --> 00:24:45,290
Next phrase, I apologize for the inconvenience.

504
00:24:45,310 --> 00:24:49,420
This is used when something causes trouble or difficulty for another person.

505
00:24:49,860 --> 00:24:50,900
Example conversation.

506
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:54,270
The online meeting started 30 minutes late yesterday.

507
00:24:54,310 --> 00:25:00,810
Yes, and I sent an email saying, I apologize for the confusion caused by the technical issue.

508
00:25:01,210 --> 00:25:02,840
Excellent professional apology.

509
00:25:03,190 --> 00:25:05,210
Very important in business communication.

510
00:25:05,570 --> 00:25:09,340
Here's another practical email phrase, at your earliest convenience.

511
00:25:09,840 --> 00:25:12,600
This means when you have time as soon as possible.

512
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:14,060
Example conversation.

513
00:25:14,520 --> 00:25:18,030
How can I politely ask the manager to review the report soon?

514
00:25:18,470 --> 00:25:23,160
You can write, please review the attached document at your earliest convenience.

515
00:25:23,180 --> 00:25:25,760
Much more professional than simply saying quickly.

516
00:25:26,220 --> 00:25:26,700
Exactly.

517
00:25:27,180 --> 00:25:29,460
Next phrase, I'm happy to assist you.

518
00:25:29,870 --> 00:25:31,830
This means you're willing to help someone.

519
00:25:32,060 --> 00:25:33,240
Example conversation.

520
00:25:33,640 --> 00:25:36,310
The new employee had trouble understanding the system.

521
00:25:36,810 --> 00:25:41,170
So I emailed her saying, I'm happy to assist you if you need any support.

522
00:25:41,660 --> 00:25:43,160
Very kind and professional.

523
00:25:43,540 --> 00:25:44,900
Great teamwork language too.

524
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:45,820
Definitely.

525
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:48,710
Next phrase, I'm reaching out regarding.

526
00:25:49,090 --> 00:25:52,370
This means you're contacting someone about a specific topic.

527
00:25:52,850 --> 00:25:53,910
Example conversation.

528
00:25:54,270 --> 00:25:56,960
Why did the marketing department contact the client again?

529
00:25:57,420 --> 00:26:01,550
They wrote, I'm reaching out regarding the upcoming product launch event.

530
00:26:01,730 --> 00:26:03,290
Very modern business English.

531
00:26:03,790 --> 00:26:05,930
Extremely common in professional emails.

532
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:07,680
Here's another excellent phrase.

533
00:26:08,180 --> 00:26:10,140
Thank you for bringing this to my attention.

534
00:26:10,600 --> 00:26:14,450
This means thanking someone for informing you about a problem or issue.

535
00:26:14,890 --> 00:26:15,970
Example conversation.

536
00:26:16,490 --> 00:26:19,150
A customer complained about an error in the invoice.

537
00:26:19,670 --> 00:26:22,940
So, I replied, thank you for bringing this to my attention.

538
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:25,000
We'll resolve the issue immediately.

539
00:26:25,380 --> 00:26:27,270
Excellent customer service communication.

540
00:26:27,310 --> 00:26:28,350
Absolutely.

541
00:26:28,790 --> 00:26:31,530
Next phrase, I look forward to hearing from you.

542
00:26:31,710 --> 00:26:35,180
This is a polite way to end an email when expecting a response.

543
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:36,740
Example conversation.

544
00:26:37,180 --> 00:26:39,360
I just applied for a partnership opportunity.

545
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:40,060
Nice.

546
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:42,150
Did you close the email professionally?

547
00:26:42,510 --> 00:26:42,790
Yes.

548
00:26:43,070 --> 00:26:45,490
I wrote, I look forward to hearing from you soon.

549
00:26:45,930 --> 00:26:47,130
Perfect closing sentence.

550
00:26:47,590 --> 00:26:49,210
Very common in formal emails.

551
00:26:49,690 --> 00:26:51,430
Here's another useful business phrase.

552
00:26:51,920 --> 00:26:53,920
Please don't hesitate to contact me.

553
00:26:54,320 --> 00:26:57,940
This means the person should feel comfortable contacting you anytime.

554
00:26:58,090 --> 00:26:59,330
Example conversation.

555
00:26:59,370 --> 00:27:02,580
The client might still have questions about the proposal.

556
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:08,190
Then end your email with, please don't hesitate to contact me if you need further information.

557
00:27:08,610 --> 00:27:10,090
Very polite and professional.

558
00:27:10,470 --> 00:27:10,990
Definitely.

559
00:27:11,390 --> 00:27:14,020
Next phrase, as discussed during the meeting.

560
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:18,220
This phrase connects the email to a previous conversation or meeting.

561
00:27:18,610 --> 00:27:19,730
Example conversation.

562
00:27:19,790 --> 00:27:22,290
Did you summarize the meeting in your email?

563
00:27:22,540 --> 00:27:27,990
Yes, I started with, as discussed during the meeting, we'll begin the project next Monday.

564
00:27:28,370 --> 00:27:29,450
Excellent business writing.

565
00:27:29,970 --> 00:27:31,610
Very clear and organized, too.

566
00:27:32,030 --> 00:27:34,440
Next phrase, I'd like to schedule a meeting.

567
00:27:34,880 --> 00:27:36,860
This means arranging a time to meet.

568
00:27:37,300 --> 00:27:41,020
Example conversation, we still need to discuss the budget proposal.

569
00:27:41,480 --> 00:27:46,490
Then send an email saying, I'd like to schedule a meeting to review the financial plan.

570
00:27:46,830 --> 00:27:47,610
Sounds professional.

571
00:27:48,050 --> 00:27:48,570
Absolutely.

572
00:27:48,890 --> 00:27:52,300
Here's another practical phrase, thank you for your patience.

573
00:27:52,430 --> 00:27:56,790
This phrase is used when someone waited calmly during a delay or problem.

574
00:27:57,190 --> 00:27:58,320
Example conversation.

575
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:01,380
The software issue took two days to fix.

576
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:02,120
Right.

577
00:28:02,380 --> 00:28:07,410
So we emailed the clients saying, thank you for your patience while we resolved the issue.

578
00:28:07,850 --> 00:28:09,890
Very professional customer communication.

579
00:28:09,930 --> 00:28:10,810
Definitely.

580
00:28:11,210 --> 00:28:11,810
Next phrase.

581
00:28:12,310 --> 00:28:14,040
Unfortunately, we are unable to.

582
00:28:14,100 --> 00:28:17,980
This is a polite way to say no or reject a request.

583
00:28:18,110 --> 00:28:19,370
Example conversation.

584
00:28:19,390 --> 00:28:23,440
The customer requested a refund outside the policy period.

585
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:28,760
So, we replied, unfortunately, we are unable to process the refund at this time.

586
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:30,200
Polite, but clear.

587
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:31,150
Exactly.

588
00:28:31,510 --> 00:28:32,790
Here's another useful phrase.

589
00:28:33,270 --> 00:28:34,510
Please keep me updated.

590
00:28:34,930 --> 00:28:38,490
This means you want regular updates about progress or changes.

591
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:40,040
Example conversation.

592
00:28:40,660 --> 00:28:43,760
The design team is still working on the advertisement campaign.

593
00:28:44,220 --> 00:28:44,580
All right.

594
00:28:44,860 --> 00:28:47,660
Please keep me updated on any major developments.

595
00:28:47,800 --> 00:28:48,940
Great management phrase.

596
00:28:49,400 --> 00:28:51,280
Very common in project communication.

597
00:28:51,640 --> 00:28:55,070
Next phrase, I'm available if you'd like to discuss this further.

598
00:28:55,530 --> 00:28:59,070
This means you're open to having a deeper conversation about the topic.

599
00:28:59,490 --> 00:29:00,570
Example conversation.

600
00:29:00,610 --> 00:29:04,000
The client seemed unsure about the proposal details.

601
00:29:04,520 --> 00:29:08,310
Then write, I'm available if you'd like to discuss this further in a meeting.

602
00:29:08,750 --> 00:29:10,110
Excellent professional tone.

603
00:29:10,510 --> 00:29:11,030
Definitely.

604
00:29:11,350 --> 00:29:13,190
Here's another important email phrase.

605
00:29:13,670 --> 00:29:16,100
Please accept my sincere apologies.

606
00:29:16,310 --> 00:29:19,260
This is a very formal and respectful apology.

607
00:29:19,700 --> 00:29:20,840
Example conversation.

608
00:29:20,880 --> 00:29:25,450
The company accidentally sent incorrect pricing information to customers.

609
00:29:25,970 --> 00:29:31,740
So the manager emailed everyone saying, please accept my sincere apologies for the confusion.

610
00:29:32,120 --> 00:29:34,120
Very professional apology language.

611
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:35,180
Absolutely.

612
00:29:35,580 --> 00:29:38,370
Next phrase, I'd like to kindly remind you.

613
00:29:38,810 --> 00:29:42,030
This phrase politely reminds someone about something important.

614
00:29:42,220 --> 00:29:43,440
Example conversation.

615
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,640
Some employees forgot to submit their weekly reports.

616
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:53,130
So send a reminder email saying, I'd like to kindly remind you that reports are due by Friday.

617
00:29:53,470 --> 00:29:54,790
Polite, but effective.

618
00:29:55,250 --> 00:29:55,670
Exactly.

619
00:29:55,970 --> 00:29:56,890
Here's a great phrase.

620
00:29:57,370 --> 00:29:59,130
Thank you for your continued support.

621
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:03,480
This means appreciating someone's ongoing help or partnership.

622
00:30:03,500 --> 00:30:04,600
Example conversation.

623
00:30:04,620 --> 00:30:08,550
The company completed 10 years working with the same client.

624
00:30:08,680 --> 00:30:14,180
That's why the CEO wrote, Thank you for your continued support and partnership over the years.

625
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:16,410
Excellent relationship-building language.

626
00:30:16,810 --> 00:30:17,330
Definitely.

627
00:30:17,670 --> 00:30:21,290
Wow, today we practiced so many professional email phrases.

628
00:30:21,790 --> 00:30:27,040
And these are all expressions people use every day in real international business environments.

629
00:30:27,480 --> 00:30:32,380
Learning professional email English can completely change the way you communicate at work.

630
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:36,170
Your emails will sound more confident, polite, and professional.

631
00:30:36,340 --> 00:30:39,300
Try using a few of these phrases in your next email at work.

632
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:45,440
Start small, practice regularly, and soon writing business emails in English will feel natural.

633
00:30:45,770 --> 00:30:48,390
Thank you so much for spending time learning with us today.

634
00:30:48,770 --> 00:30:53,270
We hope this podcast helps you communicate more confidently in your professional life.

635
00:30:53,670 --> 00:30:58,200
Keep practicing, stay motivated, and continue improving your English every day.

636
00:30:58,220 --> 00:30:59,840
Thanks again for listening, everyone.

637
00:31:00,020 --> 00:31:03,730
We'll see you next time with another practical business English podcast.

638
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:07,890
Good morning, and welcome to today's Business English training session.

639
00:31:08,300 --> 00:31:17,040
In this episode, we are focusing on one of the most important skills in the modern workplace, professional email phrases for business conversations.

640
00:31:17,380 --> 00:31:18,140
Hello, everyone.

641
00:31:18,180 --> 00:31:22,000
Email is still the backbone of professional communication.

642
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:29,110
Whether you're talking to clients, managers, or colleagues, the way you write emails reflects your professionalism.

643
00:31:29,170 --> 00:31:38,020
Today, we will walk through real email phrases, their meanings, and how they are used in practical workplace conversations and situations.

644
00:31:38,660 --> 00:31:39,260
Let's begin.

645
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:42,710
Let's start with a very common opening phrase.

646
00:31:43,250 --> 00:31:45,610
I hope this email finds you well.

647
00:31:45,590 --> 00:31:51,780
Meaning, this is a polite and formal way to start an email, showing respect and professionalism.

648
00:31:52,400 --> 00:31:54,010
Example usage in email.

649
00:31:54,630 --> 00:31:56,290
I hope this email finds you well.

650
00:31:56,850 --> 00:32:01,260
I am writing to follow up on our previous discussion regarding the project timeline.

651
00:32:01,820 --> 00:32:03,090
Conversation example.

652
00:32:03,810 --> 00:32:05,890
Did you send the email to the client?

653
00:32:06,090 --> 00:32:11,540
Yes, I started with, I hope this email finds you well to keep it professional and polite.

654
00:32:12,180 --> 00:32:14,480
Next phrase, I am writing to.

655
00:32:15,070 --> 00:32:18,190
Meaning, this clearly states the purpose of your email.

656
00:32:18,770 --> 00:32:23,240
Example usage, I am writing to request an update on the payment status.

657
00:32:23,820 --> 00:32:26,860
Conversation example, how did you start the email?

658
00:32:27,460 --> 00:32:31,630
I wrote, I am writing to confirm the meeting schedule for next week.

659
00:32:31,830 --> 00:32:34,990
Another important phrase, please find attached.

660
00:32:35,600 --> 00:32:39,530
Meaning, this is used when you are sending a document with the email.

661
00:32:40,030 --> 00:32:44,820
Example usage, please find attached the revised proposal for your review.

662
00:32:45,400 --> 00:32:48,890
Conversation example, did you send the report?

663
00:32:48,870 --> 00:32:53,640
Yes, I said, please find attached the final version of the sales report.

664
00:32:54,220 --> 00:32:56,240
Next phrase, could you please?

665
00:32:56,920 --> 00:33:00,770
Meaning, a polite way to make a request in email communication.

666
00:33:01,390 --> 00:33:06,460
Example usage, could you please review the document and share your feedback by Friday?

667
00:33:06,980 --> 00:33:11,170
Conversation example, how did you ask the manager for approval?

668
00:33:11,150 --> 00:33:16,520
I wrote, could you please approve the budget proposal at your earliest convenience?

669
00:33:16,540 --> 00:33:20,460
Now a very useful phrase, at your earliest convenience.

670
00:33:21,080 --> 00:33:25,230
Meaning, this means as soon as you are able to, in a polite tone.

671
00:33:25,770 --> 00:33:29,580
Example usage, please respond at your earliest convenience.

672
00:33:30,160 --> 00:33:33,740
Conversation example, was the email too demanding?

673
00:33:34,010 --> 00:33:37,800
No, I used at your earliest convenience to keep it respectful.

674
00:33:38,360 --> 00:33:41,640
Next phrase, I would appreciate it if...

675
00:33:41,660 --> 00:33:44,910
Meaning, a formal and polite way to request something.

676
00:33:45,470 --> 00:33:50,380
Example usage, I would appreciate it if you could share the updated report today.

677
00:33:50,920 --> 00:33:54,720
Conversation example, how did you make the request sound polite?

678
00:33:55,380 --> 00:33:59,630
I wrote, I would appreciate it if you could prioritize this task.

679
00:33:59,690 --> 00:34:03,120
Another important phrase, thank you for your prompt response.

680
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:07,050
Meaning, this is used to appreciate quick replies.

681
00:34:07,670 --> 00:34:12,110
Example usage, thank you for your prompt response regarding the meeting schedule.

682
00:34:12,630 --> 00:34:16,020
Conversation example, did the client reply quickly?

683
00:34:16,040 --> 00:34:20,840
Yes, I thanked them by saying, thank you for your prompt reply.

684
00:34:21,140 --> 00:34:23,530
Next phrase, following up on.

685
00:34:24,190 --> 00:34:27,380
Used when checking the status of something previously discussed.

686
00:34:27,960 --> 00:34:32,580
Example usage, following up on my previous email regarding the invoice.

687
00:34:33,140 --> 00:34:36,230
Conversation example, did you send a reminder email?

688
00:34:36,730 --> 00:34:41,480
Yes, I wrote following up on our earlier discussion about the contract.

689
00:34:41,700 --> 00:34:44,880
Another useful phrase, just a gentle reminder.

690
00:34:45,460 --> 00:34:48,770
Meaning, a polite way to remind someone about something.

691
00:34:49,370 --> 00:34:53,570
Example usage, just a gentle reminder that the deadline is tomorrow.

692
00:34:54,090 --> 00:34:57,460
Conversation example, how did you remind the team?

693
00:34:57,480 --> 00:35:02,320
I wrote, just a gentle reminder about submitting the monthly report.

694
00:35:02,500 --> 00:35:06,030
Now let's look at, I would like to bring to your attention.

695
00:35:06,570 --> 00:35:10,440
Meaning, use to politely highlight an issue or concern.

696
00:35:10,500 --> 00:35:16,500
Example usage, I would like to bring to your attention a delay in the project timeline.

697
00:35:16,830 --> 00:35:19,910
Conversation example, how did you report the issue?

698
00:35:20,610 --> 00:35:25,120
I wrote, I would like to bring to your attention a technical error in the system.

699
00:35:25,620 --> 00:35:27,820
Next phrase, please let me know if.

700
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:32,500
Meaning, used to offer help or request confirmation.

701
00:35:33,110 --> 00:35:37,230
Example usage, please let me know if you need any further information.

702
00:35:37,770 --> 00:35:41,630
Conversation example, did you offer assistance in your email?

703
00:35:42,050 --> 00:35:46,420
Yes, I wrote, please let me know if I can be of any assistance.

704
00:35:46,400 --> 00:35:50,660
Now let's look at options like, I look forward to your response.

705
00:35:51,350 --> 00:35:55,270
Meaning, a polite closing phrase showing expectation of reply.

706
00:35:55,310 --> 00:35:59,920
Example usage, I look forward to your response regarding the proposal.

707
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:03,430
Conversation example, how did you end the email?

708
00:36:04,070 --> 00:36:07,460
I wrote, I look forward to your response and feedback.

709
00:36:07,730 --> 00:36:11,980
Let's move to a slightly more formal phrase, kindly note that.

710
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:16,230
Meaning, used to highlight important information politely.

711
00:36:16,270 --> 00:36:21,360
Example usage, kindly note that the office will be closed on Friday.

712
00:36:21,940 --> 00:36:24,910
Conversation example, did you inform the team?

713
00:36:25,210 --> 00:36:29,210
Yes, I wrote, kindly note that the deadline has been extended.

714
00:36:29,270 --> 00:36:33,720
Now a very useful phrase, I regret to inform you.

715
00:36:34,280 --> 00:36:37,640
Meaning, used when delivering bad news professionally.

716
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:43,650
Example usage, I regret to inform you that your application was not successful.

717
00:36:44,150 --> 00:36:47,990
Conversation example, how did HR respond to the candidate?

718
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:52,750
They wrote, I regret to inform you that we are unable to proceed.

719
00:36:53,330 --> 00:36:56,110
Another phrase, thank you for your understanding.

720
00:36:56,780 --> 00:37:00,620
Meaning used when you expect the reader to accept a situation.

721
00:37:01,190 --> 00:37:04,070
Example usage, we have rescheduled the meeting.

722
00:37:04,370 --> 00:37:05,700
Thank you for your understanding.

723
00:37:06,200 --> 00:37:07,720
Conversation example,

724
00:37:07,700 --> 00:37:09,750
Did the client accept the delay?

725
00:37:09,770 --> 00:37:14,320
Yes, they appreciated the honesty and professionalism in the email.

726
00:37:14,870 --> 00:37:19,680
Let's look at a closing phrase, best regards or kind regards.

727
00:37:19,750 --> 00:37:23,280
meaning professional ways to end an email politely.

728
00:37:23,300 --> 00:37:26,960
Example usage, best regards, John Smith.

729
00:37:27,540 --> 00:37:31,350
Conversation example, how do you usually end your emails?

730
00:37:32,090 --> 00:37:37,920
I use kind regards for formal communication and best regards for general business emails.

731
00:37:38,560 --> 00:37:42,970
Another important phrase, please do not hesitate to contact me.

732
00:37:42,950 --> 00:37:46,940
Meaning, encourages the receiver to reach out for help or questions.

733
00:37:47,540 --> 00:37:48,340
Example usage.

734
00:37:48,880 --> 00:37:52,270
Please do not hesitate to contact me if you need further assistance.

735
00:37:52,870 --> 00:37:54,030
Conversation example.

736
00:37:54,570 --> 00:37:56,090
Did you make it sound friendly?

737
00:37:56,130 --> 00:37:56,870
Yes.

738
00:37:57,390 --> 00:38:01,280
I added, please do not hesitate to contact me anytime.

739
00:38:01,410 --> 00:38:04,220
Now let's talk about a real email situation.

740
00:38:04,860 --> 00:38:07,760
Imagine you are writing to a client about a delay.

741
00:38:08,300 --> 00:38:15,480
You might say, we would like to inform you that the project timeline has been adjusted due to unforeseen circumstances.

742
00:38:15,940 --> 00:38:21,110
We sincerely apologize for any inconvenience caused and appreciate your understanding.

743
00:38:21,710 --> 00:38:25,070
In conversation, how did the client react?

744
00:38:25,160 --> 00:38:28,700
They appreciated the honesty and professionalism in the email.

745
00:38:29,360 --> 00:38:32,250
Or imagine requesting information from a colleague.

746
00:38:32,810 --> 00:38:37,750
You could write, Could you please share the updated file at your earliest convenience?

747
00:38:38,210 --> 00:38:40,660
I would appreciate your prompt support on this matter.

748
00:38:41,180 --> 00:38:42,360
Conversation example.

749
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:44,680
Was the request successful?

750
00:38:44,820 --> 00:38:48,700
Yes, they replied quickly because the email was polite and clear.

751
00:38:49,280 --> 00:38:54,910
That brings us to the end of today's session on professional email phrases for business conversations.

752
00:38:55,470 --> 00:39:00,350
Remember, good email writing is about clarity, politeness, and purpose.

753
00:39:00,970 --> 00:39:06,620
The more you practice these phrases, the more natural and confident your business communication will become.

754
00:39:07,200 --> 00:39:10,920
Thank you for joining us today, and we'll see you in the next episode.

755
00:39:10,900 --> 00:39:18,370
If there's one skill that quietly defines your professionalism at work, it's not just speaking, it's writing effective emails.

756
00:39:18,950 --> 00:39:19,570
Exactly.

757
00:39:19,870 --> 00:39:27,840
Because in most offices, emails are the first impression, the ongoing communication, and sometimes even the final decision record.

