Welcome back to Business English Conversations.

759
00:39:31,700 --> 00:39:32,260
I'm Daniel.

760
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:33,410
And I'm Sophie.

761
00:39:34,010 --> 00:39:38,430
Today's episode is Professional Email Phrases for Business Conversations.

762
00:39:38,700 --> 00:39:45,610
We'll break down real email phrases, explain what they mean, and show how they're actually used in workplace communication.

763
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:50,910
Not formal textbook English, real, natural, professional email language.

764
00:39:51,410 --> 00:39:52,090
Let's get started.

765
00:39:52,690 --> 00:39:56,260
First essential phrase, I hope this email finds you well.

766
00:39:56,840 --> 00:40:00,420
Meaning, this is a polite and professional way to start an email.

767
00:40:00,840 --> 00:40:02,760
It shows respect and friendliness.

768
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:03,360
Usage.

769
00:40:03,740 --> 00:40:09,890
It is commonly used in formal or semi-formal emails, especially when contacting clients or new colleagues.

770
00:40:10,490 --> 00:40:11,750
Example in context.

771
00:40:12,350 --> 00:40:13,820
I hope this email finds you well.

772
00:40:14,280 --> 00:40:16,880
I'm writing to follow up on our meeting last week.

773
00:40:16,900 --> 00:40:19,920
It softens the tone before you introduce the main message.

774
00:40:20,590 --> 00:40:21,190
Next phrase.

775
00:40:21,570 --> 00:40:22,930
I am writing to inform you.

776
00:40:23,410 --> 00:40:23,750
Meaning.

777
00:40:24,090 --> 00:40:27,760
This is used when you want to clearly state the purpose of your email.

778
00:40:27,870 --> 00:40:32,500
usage, it is direct, professional, and very dominant workplace communication.

779
00:40:33,020 --> 00:40:37,190
Example, I am writing to inform you that the project deadline has been extended.

780
00:40:37,890 --> 00:40:41,950
Or, I am writing to inform you about the schedule change for tomorrow's meeting.

781
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:44,100
It removes confusion right away.

782
00:40:44,680 --> 00:40:46,640
Next phrase, just a quick reminder.

783
00:40:47,100 --> 00:40:52,710
Meaning, this is used when you want to politely remind someone about something without sounding pushy.

784
00:40:52,810 --> 00:40:57,080
Usage, very useful for deadlines, meetings, or pending tasks.

785
00:40:57,680 --> 00:40:58,120
Example.

786
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:01,660
Just a quick reminder that the report is due by 5 p.m. today.

787
00:41:02,220 --> 00:41:04,130
It sounds polite instead of demanding.

788
00:41:04,730 --> 00:41:07,310
Next phrase, please find attached.

789
00:41:07,330 --> 00:41:11,700
Meaning, this is used when sending files or documents with an email.

790
00:41:11,720 --> 00:41:16,810
Usage, very common in business emails when sharing reports, proposals, or invoices.

791
00:41:17,330 --> 00:41:17,750
Example.

792
00:41:18,270 --> 00:41:21,550
Please find attached the updated sales report for your review.

793
00:41:21,800 --> 00:41:24,390
it clearly signals that there is a file included.

794
00:41:24,430 --> 00:41:27,230
Next phrase, could you please clarify?

795
00:41:27,730 --> 00:41:31,860
Meaning, this is a polite way to ask for more information or explanation.

796
00:41:32,550 --> 00:41:36,260
Usage, used when something is unclear in previous communication.

797
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:40,300
Example, could you please clarify the requirements for this task?

798
00:41:40,950 --> 00:41:44,750
Or, could you please clarify the deadline mentioned in your last email?

799
00:41:45,190 --> 00:41:47,710
It keeps communication respectful and professional.

800
00:41:48,250 --> 00:41:51,240
Next phrase, thank you for your prompt response.

801
00:41:51,320 --> 00:41:54,280
Meaning, this is used to appreciate a quick reply.

802
00:41:54,920 --> 00:41:58,710
Usage, common in client communication or time-sensitive projects.

803
00:41:59,250 --> 00:41:59,690
Example.

804
00:42:00,330 --> 00:42:01,750
Thank you for your prompt response.

805
00:42:02,070 --> 00:42:03,630
I will proceed with the next steps.

806
00:42:04,170 --> 00:42:07,040
It encourages faster communication in the future as well.

807
00:42:07,700 --> 00:42:10,220
Next phrase, I would appreciate it if you could.

808
00:42:10,780 --> 00:42:13,120
Meaning, a polite way to request something.

809
00:42:13,780 --> 00:42:17,310
Usage, used when you want to sound respectful and not demanding.

810
00:42:17,870 --> 00:42:18,590
Example.

811
00:42:18,790 --> 00:42:21,950
I would appreciate it if you could review the document by tomorrow.

812
00:42:22,430 --> 00:42:24,700
It's softer than saying, send it tomorrow.

813
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:27,200
Next phrase, following up on.

814
00:42:27,740 --> 00:42:32,100
Meaning, this is used when you are checking back on a previous email or request.

815
00:42:32,760 --> 00:42:35,240
Usage, very common in business follow-ups.

816
00:42:35,780 --> 00:42:36,210
Example.

817
00:42:36,830 --> 00:42:40,070
Following up on my previous email regarding the contract update.

818
00:42:40,610 --> 00:42:40,710
Or.

819
00:42:41,330 --> 00:42:43,190
Just following up on our meeting last week.

820
00:42:43,710 --> 00:42:46,160
It keeps communication active and professional.

821
00:42:46,740 --> 00:42:48,800
Next phrase, as discussed.

822
00:42:48,930 --> 00:42:52,650
Meaning, this refers back to a previous conversation or meeting.

823
00:42:53,310 --> 00:42:53,590
Usage.

824
00:42:53,950 --> 00:42:56,540
It helps maintain continuity in communication.

825
00:42:57,100 --> 00:42:57,520
Example.

826
00:42:58,180 --> 00:43:01,220
As discussed, we will proceed with the new marketing strategy.

827
00:43:01,700 --> 00:43:04,130
It shows alignment between email and meetings.

828
00:43:04,790 --> 00:43:05,430
Next phrase.

829
00:43:05,870 --> 00:43:07,810
Please let me know if you have any questions.

830
00:43:08,310 --> 00:43:11,640
Meaning, this is a polite way to invite further communication.

831
00:43:12,300 --> 00:43:12,600
Usage.

832
00:43:13,060 --> 00:43:15,860
Used at the end of emails to remain open and helpful.

833
00:43:16,340 --> 00:43:17,160
Example.

834
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:20,170
Please let me know if you have any questions regarding the proposal.

835
00:43:20,650 --> 00:43:22,650
It encourages engagement and clarity.

836
00:43:23,270 --> 00:43:25,710
Next phrase, looking forward to your response.

837
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:29,140
Meaning, this expresses expectation of a reply.

838
00:43:29,820 --> 00:43:32,720
Usage, very common closing in business emails.

839
00:43:33,260 --> 00:43:37,250
Example, looking forward to your response regarding the project's timeline.

840
00:43:37,770 --> 00:43:40,010
It keeps the tone positive and professional.

841
00:43:40,600 --> 00:43:43,540
Next phrase, I apologize for the inconvenience.

842
00:43:43,760 --> 00:43:47,520
Meaning, this is used to express regret for any issue caused.

843
00:43:48,220 --> 00:43:51,570
Usage, common in customer service or internal corrections.

844
00:43:52,120 --> 00:43:52,530
Example.

845
00:43:53,230 --> 00:43:56,530
I apologize for the inconvenience caused by the delayed delivery.

846
00:43:56,990 --> 00:43:59,530
It helps maintain professionalism during problems.

847
00:44:00,190 --> 00:44:02,680
Next phrase, at your earliest convenience.

848
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:06,380
Meaning, this means as soon as you can, but politely.

849
00:44:07,040 --> 00:44:10,750
Usage, used when you are requesting something without urgency pressure.

850
00:44:11,230 --> 00:44:11,910
Example.

851
00:44:12,220 --> 00:44:14,830
Please review the document at your earliest convenience.

852
00:44:15,390 --> 00:44:18,050
It's polite and respectful of the other person's time.

853
00:44:18,710 --> 00:44:20,550
Next phrase, for your reference.

854
00:44:21,140 --> 00:44:24,520
Meaning, this is used when sharing information for someone to check.

855
00:44:25,180 --> 00:44:28,120
Usage, often used when attaching documents or data.

856
00:44:28,620 --> 00:44:29,050
Example.

857
00:44:29,670 --> 00:44:32,470
For your reference, I have attached the previous meeting notes.

858
00:44:32,990 --> 00:44:34,650
It signals supporting information.

859
00:44:35,250 --> 00:44:37,260
Next phrase, kindly note that.

860
00:44:37,740 --> 00:44:41,100
Meaning, this is used to highlight important information.

861
00:44:41,250 --> 00:44:45,610
Usage, common in formal communication, especially rules or deadlines.

862
00:44:46,180 --> 00:44:46,620
Example.

863
00:44:47,240 --> 00:44:49,840
Kindly note that the office will be closed on Friday.

864
00:44:50,260 --> 00:44:51,700
It draws attention politely.

865
00:44:52,340 --> 00:44:54,750
Next phrase, we are pleased to inform you.

866
00:44:55,290 --> 00:44:57,690
Meaning, this is used to share positive news.

867
00:44:58,470 --> 00:45:01,980
Usage, often used in HR, offers or approvals.

868
00:45:02,600 --> 00:45:03,020
Example.

869
00:45:03,620 --> 00:45:06,800
We are pleased to inform you that your application has been approved.

870
00:45:07,280 --> 00:45:09,570
It creates a positive professional tone.

871
00:45:09,800 --> 00:45:12,990
Next phrase, please do not hesitate to contact me.

872
00:45:13,490 --> 00:45:16,630
Meaning, this means feel free to reach out anytime.

873
00:45:17,330 --> 00:45:19,870
Usage, used to encourage communication.

874
00:45:20,350 --> 00:45:20,800
Example.

875
00:45:21,420 --> 00:45:25,240
Please do not hesitate to contact me if you need further assistance.

876
00:45:25,260 --> 00:45:27,040
It shows openness and support.

877
00:45:27,600 --> 00:45:30,050
Next phrase, I look forward to hearing from you.

878
00:45:30,510 --> 00:45:33,330
Meaning, it expresses expectation of a reply.

879
00:45:33,370 --> 00:45:36,850
Usage, very common closing in business emails.

880
00:45:37,370 --> 00:45:38,160
Example.

881
00:45:38,320 --> 00:45:41,180
I look forward to hearing from you regarding the next steps.

882
00:45:41,680 --> 00:45:44,050
It ends emails politely and professionally.

883
00:45:44,670 --> 00:45:47,810
Next phrase, best regards or kind regards.

884
00:45:48,330 --> 00:45:51,450
Meaning, these are standard professional email closings.

885
00:45:52,200 --> 00:45:55,560
Usage, used at the end of almost all formal emails.

886
00:45:56,080 --> 00:45:56,500
Example.

887
00:45:57,080 --> 00:45:58,820
Kind regards, Sarah Johnson.

888
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:01,410
It signals the end of the message politely.

889
00:46:02,010 --> 00:46:04,930
Let's quickly put it all together in a real email flow.

890
00:46:05,370 --> 00:46:05,850
Good idea.

891
00:46:06,430 --> 00:46:07,110
Example.

892
00:46:07,310 --> 00:46:09,050
I hope this email finds you well.

893
00:46:09,380 --> 00:46:12,460
I'm writing to inform you about the updated project timeline.

894
00:46:12,900 --> 00:46:15,870
Please find attached the revised schedule for your reference.

895
00:46:16,390 --> 00:46:19,390
Kindly note that the deadline has been moved to next Friday.

896
00:46:19,910 --> 00:46:23,600
I would appreciate it if you could review the document at your earliest convenience.

897
00:46:24,080 --> 00:46:25,920
Please let me know if you have any questions.

898
00:46:26,400 --> 00:46:27,720
I look forward to hearing from you.

899
00:46:28,110 --> 00:46:29,430
Kind regards, Daniel.

900
00:46:29,990 --> 00:46:34,320
That's a complete professional email using multiple key phrases naturally.

901
00:46:34,570 --> 00:46:39,370
And once learners practice this structure, writing emails becomes much easier and faster.

902
00:46:39,930 --> 00:46:44,720
Because instead of thinking sentence by sentence, you're using ready-made professional patterns.

903
00:46:45,300 --> 00:46:45,800
Exactly.

904
00:46:45,840 --> 00:46:48,990
That's how fluency and business writing actually develops.

905
00:46:49,690 --> 00:46:50,750
Thanks for joining us today.

906
00:46:51,170 --> 00:46:53,260
Practice these phrases in your next email.

907
00:46:53,460 --> 00:46:54,980
You'll notice the difference immediately.

908
00:46:55,020 --> 00:46:58,140
And we'll see you in the next Business English episode.

909
00:46:58,550 --> 00:46:59,850
Goodbye and take care.

910
00:46:59,930 --> 00:47:04,180
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to another Business English Learning podcast.

911
00:47:04,480 --> 00:47:05,040
I'm Daniel.

912
00:47:05,260 --> 00:47:06,080
And I'm Chloe.

913
00:47:06,420 --> 00:47:16,160
We're very happy you joined us today because today's topic is extremely important for anyone who works in an office or communicates professionally in English.

914
00:47:16,420 --> 00:47:16,860
That's right.

915
00:47:17,060 --> 00:47:21,430
Today we're talking about professional email phrases for business conversations.

916
00:47:21,410 --> 00:47:24,010
Emails are a huge part of modern work life.

917
00:47:24,550 --> 00:47:31,080
Every day, people send emails to managers, coworkers, clients, customers, and business partners.

918
00:47:31,500 --> 00:47:35,370
And sometimes writing professional emails in English can feel stressful.

919
00:47:35,690 --> 00:47:38,590
Especially when you don't know the right phrases to use.

920
00:47:38,970 --> 00:47:39,520
Exactly.

921
00:47:39,740 --> 00:47:46,510
So today, we're going to help you sound more natural, professional, polite, and confident in your business emails.

922
00:47:46,490 --> 00:47:54,000
We'll teach you useful email phrases, explain their meanings, and show practical examples through real workplace conversations.

923
00:47:54,360 --> 00:47:59,280
So grab your notebook, relax, and let's start learning real business English together.

924
00:47:59,560 --> 00:48:00,060
Let's begin.

925
00:48:00,120 --> 00:48:01,390
All right, Chloe.

926
00:48:01,590 --> 00:48:02,910
Imagine this situation.

927
00:48:03,170 --> 00:48:06,450
You arrive at the office Monday morning and open your inbox.

928
00:48:06,430 --> 00:48:08,700
Hundreds of emails waiting for you already.

929
00:48:09,020 --> 00:48:09,680
Exactly.

930
00:48:09,880 --> 00:48:13,480
And the first thing many people need to do is write a professional email.

931
00:48:13,840 --> 00:48:17,350
So let's start with one of the most common email opening phrases.

932
00:48:17,770 --> 00:48:18,790
I hope you're doing well.

933
00:48:19,110 --> 00:48:20,990
This is a polite way to begin an email.

934
00:48:21,330 --> 00:48:25,120
It shows professionalism and respect before getting into business details.

935
00:48:25,520 --> 00:48:26,640
Example conversation.

936
00:48:27,000 --> 00:48:29,730
Chloe, how should I start this email to the client?

937
00:48:30,050 --> 00:48:34,090
You can write, I hope you're doing well, before discussing the project.

938
00:48:34,070 --> 00:48:35,700
Very natural and professional.

939
00:48:36,020 --> 00:48:39,520
Another common opening phrase is, I'm writing to inform you that.

940
00:48:39,880 --> 00:48:42,150
It means you are sending important information.

941
00:48:42,530 --> 00:48:43,650
Example conversation.

942
00:48:43,970 --> 00:48:46,480
What should I write when announcing schedule changes?

943
00:48:46,820 --> 00:48:51,220
You can say, I'm writing to inform you that tomorrow's meeting has been postponed.

944
00:48:51,590 --> 00:48:53,090
Excellent business communication.

945
00:48:53,370 --> 00:48:55,050
Here's another useful phrase.

946
00:48:55,070 --> 00:49:00,060
This means you are contacting someone about a specific topic.

947
00:49:00,420 --> 00:49:04,370
It sounds more modern and professional than simply saying, I'm contacting you.

948
00:49:04,750 --> 00:49:08,900
I need to ask the supplier about delayed shipments.

949
00:49:13,450 --> 00:49:15,150
Very fluent business English.

950
00:49:15,220 --> 00:49:18,200
Now, let's talk about asking for information politely.

951
00:49:18,220 --> 00:49:21,620
One excellent phrase is, could you please provide.

952
00:49:21,980 --> 00:49:25,220
This phrase sounds polite, respectful, and professional.

953
00:49:25,560 --> 00:49:26,610
Example conversation.

954
00:49:26,970 --> 00:49:30,010
I need updated sales numbers from the finance department.

955
00:49:30,350 --> 00:49:34,910
You can email them saying, could you please provide the latest sales report by Friday?

956
00:49:35,270 --> 00:49:36,550
Perfect office English.

957
00:49:36,970 --> 00:49:40,200
Another useful phrase is, I would appreciate it if.

958
00:49:40,620 --> 00:49:43,360
This is another polite way to make requests in emails.

959
00:49:43,820 --> 00:49:44,900
Example conversation.

960
00:49:45,120 --> 00:49:48,510
How can I politely ask my manager to review my presentation?

961
00:49:48,830 --> 00:49:49,090
Right.

962
00:49:49,690 --> 00:49:53,860
I would appreciate it if you could review the presentation before tomorrow's meeting.

963
00:49:53,880 --> 00:49:55,460
Professional and respectful.

964
00:49:55,800 --> 00:49:58,130
Here's another very common email phrase.

965
00:49:58,280 --> 00:50:00,200
Please find attached.

966
00:50:00,220 --> 00:50:02,490
This means you attached a file to the email.

967
00:50:02,870 --> 00:50:03,970
Example conversation.

968
00:50:04,350 --> 00:50:06,610
I'm sending the contract document to the client.

969
00:50:06,990 --> 00:50:10,880
Then write, please find attached the updated contract for your review.

970
00:50:11,260 --> 00:50:13,240
Extremely common in business emails.

971
00:50:13,600 --> 00:50:14,160
Definitely.

972
00:50:14,520 --> 00:50:15,980
Here's another useful phrase.

973
00:50:16,340 --> 00:50:17,220
For your reference.

974
00:50:17,590 --> 00:50:21,830
This means the information is being shared to help someone understand something better.

975
00:50:22,190 --> 00:50:23,310
Example conversation.

976
00:50:23,730 --> 00:50:26,020
Should I include last month's report too?

977
00:50:26,060 --> 00:50:27,580
Yes, write,

978
00:50:27,560 --> 00:50:31,350
For your reference, I've also attached last month's performance report.

979
00:50:31,670 --> 00:50:33,010
Great professional language.

980
00:50:33,390 --> 00:50:35,490
Now, let's talk about follow-up emails.

981
00:50:36,090 --> 00:50:38,600
Ah, yes, very important in business communication.

982
00:50:39,100 --> 00:50:42,110
One common phrase is, I'm following up on...

983
00:50:42,130 --> 00:50:45,840
This means you are checking the status of a previous email or discussion.

984
00:50:46,240 --> 00:50:47,560
Example conversation.

985
00:50:47,540 --> 00:50:51,100
I sent the proposal email to the client, but they haven't answered yet.

986
00:50:51,530 --> 00:50:56,950
Then send a follow-up email saying, I'm following up on my previous email regarding the proposal.

987
00:50:57,470 --> 00:51:01,660
Another excellent phrase is, just a friendly reminder.

988
00:51:02,380 --> 00:51:06,290
Example conversation.

989
00:51:06,270 --> 00:51:09,010
I need to remind the team about tomorrow's deadline.

990
00:51:09,380 --> 00:51:14,640
You can write, just a friendly reminder that the final reports are due tomorrow at 5 p.m.

991
00:51:15,010 --> 00:51:17,210
Polite and professional at the same time.

992
00:51:17,570 --> 00:51:20,590
Here's another phrase used constantly in office emails.

993
00:51:20,980 --> 00:51:23,220
Please let me know if you have any questions.

994
00:51:23,240 --> 00:51:26,090
This phrase invites communication and sounds helpful.

995
00:51:26,430 --> 00:51:27,570
Example conversation.

996
00:51:27,680 --> 00:51:30,450
Should I end the email after explaining the process?

997
00:51:30,830 --> 00:51:33,920
Yes, and add, please let me know if you have any questions.

998
00:51:34,380 --> 00:51:36,140
Excellent customer service language.

999
00:51:36,580 --> 00:51:39,150
Now let's discuss scheduling meetings through email.

1000
00:51:39,630 --> 00:51:42,640
One common phrase is, would you be available for a meeting?

1001
00:51:43,060 --> 00:51:44,460
Very polite and professional.

1002
00:51:44,800 --> 00:51:46,160
Example conversation.

1003
00:51:46,140 --> 00:51:49,310
I need to arrange a project discussion with the marketing team.

1004
00:51:49,670 --> 00:51:49,910
Right.

1005
00:51:50,290 --> 00:51:53,050
Would you be available for a meeting this Thursday afternoon?

1006
00:51:53,430 --> 00:51:54,450
Great business phrase.

1007
00:51:54,850 --> 00:51:58,060
Another useful one is, please confirm your availability.

1008
00:51:58,440 --> 00:52:01,460
Meaning, ask someone to confirm whether they are free.

1009
00:52:01,840 --> 00:52:02,940
Example conversation.

1010
00:52:03,300 --> 00:52:05,550
We need to finalize the meeting schedule today.

1011
00:52:05,590 --> 00:52:10,330
Then email everyone and say, please confirm your availability by noon.

1012
00:52:10,310 --> 00:52:12,330
Very natural office communication.

1013
00:52:12,750 --> 00:52:16,030
Now let's talk about apologizing professionally in emails.

1014
00:52:16,520 --> 00:52:17,700
Very important skill.

1015
00:52:18,140 --> 00:52:21,740
One excellent phrase is, I apologize for the inconvenience.

1016
00:52:22,100 --> 00:52:25,740
This is used when something causes trouble or difficulty for someone else.

1017
00:52:26,200 --> 00:52:27,290
Example conversation.

1018
00:52:27,670 --> 00:52:31,050
The system crashed and customers couldn't access their accounts.

1019
00:52:31,530 --> 00:52:37,200
Then send an email saying, we apologize for the inconvenience caused by the technical issue.

1020
00:52:37,180 --> 00:52:38,700
Professional and respectful.

1021
00:52:39,080 --> 00:52:42,360
Another useful phrase is, thank you for your patience.

1022
00:52:42,380 --> 00:52:45,070
This phrase is often used after delays or problems.

1023
00:52:45,470 --> 00:52:46,570
Example conversation.

1024
00:52:46,950 --> 00:52:49,000
The delivery arrived two days late.

1025
00:52:49,340 --> 00:52:53,380
Then email the client saying, thank you for your patience and understanding.

1026
00:52:53,780 --> 00:52:55,490
Excellent customer communication.

1027
00:52:55,590 --> 00:52:58,270
Now, let's discuss giving updates in emails.

1028
00:52:58,730 --> 00:53:01,760
One common phrase is, I'd like to update you on...

1029
00:53:02,160 --> 00:53:04,820
Meaning, provide new information about progress.

1030
00:53:05,270 --> 00:53:06,250
Example conversation.

1031
00:53:06,590 --> 00:53:09,450
How can I update my manager about the project status?

1032
00:53:09,900 --> 00:53:10,100
Right.

1033
00:53:10,680 --> 00:53:13,860
I'd like to update you on the progress of the marketing campaign.

1034
00:53:13,840 --> 00:53:15,410
Clear and professional.

1035
00:53:15,750 --> 00:53:18,930
Another excellent phrase is, we're currently working on...

1036
00:53:19,360 --> 00:53:21,720
This explains ongoing work or progress.

1037
00:53:22,160 --> 00:53:23,160
Example conversation.

1038
00:53:23,480 --> 00:53:26,390
The software team still hasn't finished the system upgrade.

1039
00:53:26,670 --> 00:53:30,700
You can email the client saying, we're currently working on resolving the issue.

1040
00:53:31,160 --> 00:53:33,100
Very realistic business English.

1041
00:53:33,080 --> 00:53:35,160
Here's another important email phrase.

1042
00:53:35,510 --> 00:53:36,850
We appreciate your feedback.

1043
00:53:37,190 --> 00:53:39,850
This means you value someone's opinions or comments.

1044
00:53:40,270 --> 00:53:41,430
Example conversation.

1045
00:53:41,810 --> 00:53:44,600
A customer gave suggestions for improving the service.

1046
00:53:45,000 --> 00:53:49,580
Then reply, we appreciate your feedback and will carefully review your suggestions.

1047
00:53:50,060 --> 00:53:52,830
Very professional customer relations language.

1048
00:53:52,850 --> 00:53:53,410
Absolutely.

1049
00:53:53,830 --> 00:53:56,690
Now let's talk about ending emails professionally.

1050
00:53:56,670 --> 00:53:59,840
One common phrase is looking forward to hearing from you.

1051
00:54:00,180 --> 00:54:02,880
This means you expect or hope for a reply.

1052
00:54:03,260 --> 00:54:04,400
Example conversation.

1053
00:54:04,840 --> 00:54:07,290
I sent a partnership proposal to another company.

1054
00:54:07,650 --> 00:54:10,990
Then end the email with looking forward to hearing from you soon.

1055
00:54:11,430 --> 00:54:12,570
Friendly and professional.

1056
00:54:12,970 --> 00:54:17,200
Another excellent closing phrase is thank you for your time and consideration.

1057
00:54:17,540 --> 00:54:19,500
This shows appreciation and respect.

1058
00:54:19,860 --> 00:54:21,000
Example conversation.

1059
00:54:21,440 --> 00:54:23,250
I'm applying for a management position.

1060
00:54:23,630 --> 00:54:25,230
Then end the email with

1061
00:54:25,360 --> 00:54:27,320
Thank you for your time and consideration.

1062
00:54:27,740 --> 00:54:29,510
Very polished professional English.

1063
00:54:29,890 --> 00:54:31,710
Here's another useful closing phrase.

1064
00:54:32,110 --> 00:54:33,890
Please don't hesitate to contact me.

1065
00:54:34,290 --> 00:54:36,760
Meaning, feel free to contact me anytime.

1066
00:54:37,100 --> 00:54:38,100
Example conversation.

1067
00:54:38,440 --> 00:54:40,820
Should I offer further assistance to the client?

1068
00:54:41,140 --> 00:54:42,040
Yes, right.

1069
00:54:42,460 --> 00:54:45,890
Please don't hesitate to contact me if you need additional support.

1070
00:54:46,310 --> 00:54:47,890
Excellent client communication.

1071
00:54:48,110 --> 00:54:51,010
Here's another important phrase used in team emails.

1072
00:54:51,470 --> 00:54:52,620
As discussed in the meeting.

1073
00:54:52,640 --> 00:54:55,970
This refers to information already talked about earlier.

1074
00:54:56,350 --> 00:54:57,470
Example conversation.

1075
00:54:57,910 --> 00:55:00,280
I need to summarize today's meeting in an email.

1076
00:55:00,620 --> 00:55:05,450
Then start with, as discussed in the meeting, we'll begin the new project next Monday.

1077
00:55:05,870 --> 00:55:07,550
Very common in workplaces.

1078
00:55:07,530 --> 00:55:09,900
Here's another phrase professionals use daily.

1079
00:55:10,220 --> 00:55:11,460
Please keep me updated.

1080
00:55:11,820 --> 00:55:14,810
Meaning continue sharing new information or progress.

1081
00:55:15,230 --> 00:55:16,230
Example conversation.

1082
00:55:16,590 --> 00:55:18,960
The IT team is fixing the server problem.

1083
00:55:19,380 --> 00:55:19,760
All right.

1084
00:55:20,180 --> 00:55:21,970
Please keep me updated on the progress.

1085
00:55:22,450 --> 00:55:24,170
Very natural office English.

1086
00:55:24,150 --> 00:55:28,240
Another strong business phrase is, we're pleased to announce.

1087
00:55:28,260 --> 00:55:30,760
This phrase is used for positive company news.

1088
00:55:31,180 --> 00:55:32,210
Example conversation.

1089
00:55:32,530 --> 00:55:34,970
The company is opening a new office overseas.

1090
00:55:35,410 --> 00:55:40,680
Then email employees saying, we're pleased to announce the opening of our new Singapore office.

1091
00:55:41,060 --> 00:55:42,620
Exciting and professional.

1092
00:55:42,970 --> 00:55:44,830
Here's another excellent phrase.

1093
00:55:44,810 --> 00:55:48,170
Please review the document at your earliest convenience.

1094
00:55:48,190 --> 00:55:51,460
Meaning, review the document as soon as reasonably possible.

1095
00:55:51,820 --> 00:55:52,940
Example conversation.

1096
00:55:53,320 --> 00:55:55,910
The legal department needs approval for the contract.

1097
00:55:56,270 --> 00:56:00,910
Then write, please review the attached document at your earliest convenience.

1098
00:56:00,930 --> 00:56:02,550
Very professional email wording.

1099
00:56:02,900 --> 00:56:03,440
Definitely.

1100
00:56:03,800 --> 00:56:05,220
Here's another useful phrase.

1101
00:56:05,600 --> 00:56:07,640
Thank you for bringing this to my attention.

1102
00:56:07,620 --> 00:56:11,350
This phrase is often used when someone reports a problem or concern.

1103
00:56:11,750 --> 00:56:12,850
Example conversation.

1104
00:56:13,190 --> 00:56:15,990
An employee noticed incorrect information in the report.

1105
00:56:16,290 --> 00:56:19,120
Then reply, thank you for bringing this to my attention.

1106
00:56:19,480 --> 00:56:20,780
I'll look into it immediately.

1107
00:56:21,200 --> 00:56:22,820
Excellent leadership communication.

1108
00:56:23,200 --> 00:56:24,610
Here's another common phrase.

1109
00:56:25,050 --> 00:56:27,290
Please accept my sincere apologies.

1110
00:56:27,670 --> 00:56:30,090
This is a more formal and stronger apology.

1111
00:56:30,470 --> 00:56:31,470
Example conversation.

1112
00:56:31,810 --> 00:56:34,780
The company accidentally charged the customer twice.

1113
00:56:34,960 --> 00:56:39,010
Then write, please accept my sincere apologies for the billing error.

1114
00:56:39,410 --> 00:56:41,530
Very professional customer service English.

1115
00:56:41,890 --> 00:56:45,900
Wow, Chloe, today's lesson was packed with useful business email phrases.

1116
00:56:46,360 --> 00:56:47,000
Absolutely.

1117
00:56:47,020 --> 00:56:51,670
These are phrases professionals use every day in real companies around the world.

1118
00:56:51,650 --> 00:56:55,320
And the more you practice them, the more natural your English will sound.

1119
00:56:55,700 --> 00:56:59,200
Remember, everyone, professional emails are not only about grammar.

1120
00:56:59,490 --> 00:57:03,270
They're also about tone, politeness, clarity, and confidence.

1121
00:57:03,690 --> 00:57:09,000
So try using these phrases in your own emails at work, school, or business communication.

1122
00:57:09,430 --> 00:57:13,790
Even practicing a few phrases every day can improve your business English quickly.

1123
00:57:13,770 --> 00:57:17,920
Take care, everyone.

1124
00:57:18,140 --> 00:57:30,040
Bye, everybody.

1125
00:57:45,990 --> 00:57:48,990
Thank you.
